  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 350 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThe same upper-level trough and surface low pressure system \nmentioned in the short term discussion will bring an end to the warm \nweather this weekend as a cold front drives through the region \nSaturday night. Ensemble guidance continues to show very high \nconfidence in widespread light rain with the frontal passage with \nprobabilities of measurable precipitation at 100% nearly areawide. \nThe potential decreases negligibly with southeastern extent with the \nloss of upper level forcing, but even then probabilities still \nremain at about 90%. Increase the threshold to 0.20\" over 24 \nhours, and the difference becomes more apparent. KIRK is still \nhighlighted in a 90% probability, while locations like KFAM and \nKSAR only peak at about 65%. \n\nThe temperature forecast on Sunday will be split northwest to \nsoutheast, with exact values depending on the true timing of the \ncold front. Highs in portions of northeast Missouri and west-central \nIllinois likely won't climb much higher than their sunrise \ntemperatures (low 50s), whereas locations ahead of the front are \nforecast to reach the mid-60s. Everyone will feel the cooldown next \nweek though, as post-frontal cool air truly reaches the Mid-\nMississippi Valley. This is well reflected in ensemble guidance. The \nprobability of temperatures cooler than 50 degrees for Monday \nthrough Wednesday are solidly 100% areawide on the GEFS, GEPS, and \nENS. In short, this cold front will take our temperatures from 20-25 \ndegrees above normal on Saturday to 5-10 degrees below normal by \nTuesday.",
